## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical idempotency testing in a distributed system, covering state stores, event emission, concurrency, and failure modes.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotent operations and deduplication windows
- Durable storage guarantees and TTL management
- Concurrency control and race conditions
- Failure modes and graceful degradation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-handler: on POST /orders with idempotencyKey
if (!store.has(key)) {
  const order = createOrder(payload);
  publishKafka(order);
  store.set(key, order.id, ttl);
  return { status: 200, body: { orderId: order.id } };
} else {
  return { status: 200, body: { orderId: store.get(key) } };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test TTL-based key reuse vs. hard deduplication windows?
- How would you monitor and alert on rising duplicate requests or stuck idempotency keys?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for REST + streaming under multi-tenant data PODs with failure modes are common in high-scale apps; this checks reliability, isolation, and observability.

## Key Concepts
- Contract testing (Pact/OpenAPI)
- Cursor pagination correctness
- Streaming dedup / backpressure
- Fault injection / resilience
- Multi-tenant data isolation and security
- Observability and tracing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-test: subscribe to /stream and verify deduplicated events
const stream = fetchStream(`/users/${uid}/transactions/stream`);
const seen = new Set();
for await (const evt of stream) {
  if (seen.has(evt.id)) throw new Error('Dedup');
  seen.add(evt.id);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you simulate partial upstream outages in CI vs. staging?
- How would you verify per-tenant isolation with noisy neighbors?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a real-world asynchronous export flow requiring idempotency, retry logic, and observability. It validates high-concurrency behavior and failure modes across the message broker and downstream analytics partner.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotent POST endpoints with deduplication keys
- At-least-once vs exactly-once processing semantics
- End-to-end observability and monitoring
- Exponential backoff retry strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example test skeleton for idempotent export
async function testIdempotentExport(exportId) {
  const res1 = await post('/exports', {
    user_id: 'user123',
    export_options: { format: 'csv', date_range: '30d' },
    export_id: exportId
  });

  const res2 = await post('/exports', {
    user_id: 'user123',
    export_options: { format: 'csv', date_range: '30d' },
    export_id: exportId
  });

  // First request should queue the export
  assert(res1.status === 202);
  // Second request should return existing export
  assert(res2.status === 200 && res2.data.export_id === exportId);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Why: testing streaming APIs with multi-tenant quotas and graceful degradation is common in fintech and data platforms; it surfaces contract testing, latency guarantees, and resilience.

## Key Concepts
- SSE streaming contract, idempotency, replay protection
- Tenant isolation under burst traffic
- Latency percentiles and fallback paths
- End-to-end tooling integration (contract tests, load tests, mocks)

## Code Example
